<h2>Get a carton-level packing list before arrival</h2>
<p>Ask the supplier to identify the shipment, carton IDs and the SKU, colour, size and quantity inside each carton. Where the receiver uses an advance shipping notice (ASN), agree how carton or pallet identifiers will match it. The <a href="https://www.gs1.org/docs/tl/GS1_Logistic_Label_Guideline.pdf">GS1 Logistic Label Guideline</a> explains the relationship between logistics labels, despatch information and receiving checks. For a simple order without an ASN, a carton-numbered spreadsheet can serve the same reconciliation task.</p>
<h2>Distinguish short shipment, short delivery and mispacking</h2>
<p>Consider a hypothetical order of 120 sets. The final list says 100 were packed for this shipment and 20 are scheduled separately. If the warehouse counts 98, there are two units to investigate against the shipment and 20 outstanding against the order. If a carton never arrived, reconcile the shipment handover before opening a product shortage case. If all cartons arrived, count the affected variants instead of relying on gross weight or the overall total.</p>
<p>A warehouse can count the expected total and still be short of the right size. If a carton lists 12 black M leggings but contains ten black M and two black L, record a shortage of two M and an overage of two L. Avoid silently netting the sizes together. For a prepacked set, check whether both pieces are present and match the pack label before recording the set as sellable stock.</p>
<h2>Send a discrepancy note the supplier can act on</h2>
<p>Use one row per affected carton and SKU: PO, shipment reference, carton ID, expected count, received count, issue type and evidence filename. Attach the carton label, the relevant packing list row, and a photo or recount record. Keep packaging until the cause and next step are agreed. Check the notification window under your supplier and carrier terms, then request a written disposition such as a recount, split-shipment confirmation or another agreed resolution.</p>
<p>For a future Fitclo order, share your receiving template and carton labelling requirements during <a href="/inquiry/">the quotation discussion</a>. Define the handoff before shipment. The <a href="/blogs/activewear-quality-inspection-checklist/">pre-shipment inspection guide</a> covers upstream checks; this checklist begins when the goods reach your warehouse.</p>
